  "body": "## Root Cause\nThe audio engine was originally hardcoded to initialize the `Equalizer` and `BassBoost` with `Session 0` (the deprecated global mix), failing to attach to valid third-party playback apps like Spotify. Furthermore, the UI claimed the engine was `Connected` merely because effect classes constructed without throwing an exception, without validating true audio session control or waiting for broadcast signals.\n\n## Architecture Implemented\n- Created `WaveForgeAudioService` to stay alive and listen for `AudioEffect.ACTION_OPEN_AUDIO_EFFECT_CONTROL_SESSION` and `ACTION_CLOSE_AUDIO_EFFECT_CONTROL_SESSION` broadcasts.\n- Modified `AudioEngine` to act on these specific callbacks instead of forcing a global connection on initialization. It now extracts the actual Session ID and Package Name.\n- Transitioned `engineState` to a typed `EngineState` sealed class to distinctly represent `Attached`, `WaitingForSession`, `Disabled`, and `Error`.\n- Integrated validation to ensure WaveForge actually acquired control (`hasControl()`) over the session.\n- Added a dedicated Diagnostics panel in Settings to copy state values for debugging.\n\n## Files Changed\n- `WaveForgeApplication.kt`\n- `WaveForgeAudioService.kt`\n- `AudioEngine.kt`\n- `WaveForgeViewModel.kt`\n- `DashboardScreen.kt`, `SettingsScreen.kt`, `EqualizerScreen.kt`\n- `AndroidManifest.xml`\n\n## Limitations\nSome OEMs silently restrict global audio control. If Spotify/Android does not emit the `OPEN_AUDIO_EFFECT_CONTROL_SESSION` broadcast, WaveForge will gracefully log and display `Waiting for compatible audio session` in the UI.\n\n## Build & Test Status\n- **Build Result**: SUCCESS.\n- **Test Status**: Audible processing successfully verifies when attached to an exposed active session.",
